WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
The Jack Benny Program.

00:07.000 --> 00:23.400
Thank you very, very much, ladies and gentlemen.

00:23.400 --> 00:28.880
As you probably know, this is my first television show of the season.

00:28.880 --> 00:33.400
And I want everybody at home who's watching the show to sort of sit back and relax and

00:33.400 --> 00:36.720
take it easy because this is a very simple show, nothing to it, you know.

00:36.720 --> 00:39.560
There's been no commotion backstage at all.

00:39.560 --> 00:41.960
I mean, nobody has worked hard.

00:41.960 --> 00:44.200
That is nobody but my makeup man.

00:44.200 --> 00:46.200
He's exhausted.

00:46.200 --> 00:53.000
If I had the makeup on my shoulders that he put on my face, I could play the hunchback

00:53.000 --> 00:56.680
of Notre Dame.

00:56.680 --> 01:02.360
But I would like to tell you something of my plans for television this season.

01:02.360 --> 01:07.560
I'm going to have guests on my program, some from my radio show, and then some very, very

01:07.560 --> 01:10.520
important guest stars from the movies.

01:10.520 --> 01:16.320
Of course, on my radio show, you see, it's very difficult for me to get guest stars

01:16.320 --> 01:20.240
because, well, I don't pay much.

01:20.240 --> 01:23.200
I mean, there's no reason for it.

01:23.200 --> 01:26.080
I just don't believe in it, you see.

01:26.080 --> 01:29.040
But you see, in television, it's entirely different because a lot of people like to

01:29.040 --> 01:30.360
come on and be seen.

01:30.360 --> 01:35.880
Now, on my second show, which is about six weeks from now, oh, I have some of the most

01:35.880 --> 01:38.760
wonderful entertainers that you've ever seen in your life.

01:38.760 --> 01:43.960
I can't mention their names right now because I haven't got their contracts signed yet,

01:43.960 --> 01:44.960
you see.

01:44.960 --> 01:52.480
But if you like train seals, don't miss this.

01:52.480 --> 01:54.440
These seals are simply wonderful.

01:54.440 --> 01:58.000
We've got one seal, one of these train seals, that does a trick.

01:58.000 --> 02:03.760
He balances a basketball on his nose, you know, and keeps balancing it and twirling

02:03.760 --> 02:05.480
it and bouncing it up and down.

02:05.480 --> 02:06.480
He's amazing.

02:06.480 --> 02:11.960
Well, I tell you, this seal is so good with this basketball that he was already offered

02:11.960 --> 02:19.200
$500 to throw the show.

02:19.200 --> 02:24.320
But on this show, my guests on this show are, of course, you know, I've been rehearsing

02:24.320 --> 02:25.920
so much, I get a little bit confused.

02:25.920 --> 02:27.160
Oh, hello, boys.

02:27.160 --> 02:35.520
Oh, yes, I have the Park Avenue Hillbilly, Dorothy Shea, and Frank Remley, and Don Wilson.

02:35.520 --> 02:38.260
Those are my three guests here.

02:38.260 --> 02:40.200
And so first, I'd like to introduce...

02:40.200 --> 02:43.200
Oh, before I do, I've got to tell you one joke, a story.

02:43.200 --> 02:46.280
As a rule, I don't tell jokes, you know, I just talk, you know.

02:46.280 --> 02:49.840
But this is a joke that I heard when I was playing at the Palladium Theatre in London.

02:49.840 --> 02:55.120
It's about an actor who was standing in front of the Palladium in London, and a fellow walked

02:55.120 --> 02:56.120
over to him.

02:56.120 --> 02:59.040
And this actor was very blue and very dejected.

02:59.040 --> 03:02.520
And a friend of his walked over to him, and he said, I say, Derek...

03:02.520 --> 03:07.280
See, most of the actors in London are called Derek, you know.

03:07.280 --> 03:12.200
They have an occasional Basil or a Sidney or a Cecil, but Derek seems to be the popular

03:12.200 --> 03:13.560
name there.

03:13.560 --> 03:17.160
So this fellow walked over to this actor, and he said to him, Derek, what's the matter

03:17.160 --> 03:18.160
with you?

03:18.160 --> 03:19.160
You look so blue.

03:19.160 --> 03:20.160
He said, oh, it's nothing.

03:20.160 --> 03:21.160
He said, well, there must be something the matter with you.

03:21.160 --> 03:22.160
Now, what is it?

03:22.160 --> 03:25.120
So this actor said, he said, well, let me tell you what happened.

03:25.120 --> 03:31.520
He said, about three weeks ago, an uncle of mine died and left me 10,000 pounds.

03:31.520 --> 03:37.360
He says, two weeks ago, another uncle of mine died and left me 5,000 pounds.

03:37.360 --> 03:38.360
Now last week...

03:38.360 --> 03:39.360
Stage manager.

03:39.360 --> 03:40.360
Last week...

03:40.360 --> 03:46.120
I wonder if you'd draw those travelers and bring down my scenery.

03:46.120 --> 03:47.120
Yeah.

03:47.120 --> 03:48.120
Thank you.

03:48.120 --> 03:49.120
Would you give this music to Mr. Merrick, please?

03:49.120 --> 03:50.120
Thank you.

03:50.120 --> 04:01.120
Ladies and gentlemen, a few weeks ago, I was on the Alan Young Show, and I sang a song...

04:01.120 --> 04:09.440
The response was so wonderful that I'd like to do an encore for you tonight, if you will,

04:09.440 --> 04:13.040
with my wife, Mr. Merrick.

04:13.040 --> 04:14.960
Thank you.

04:14.960 --> 04:26.920
I'm sending you a big bouquet of roses, one for every time you broke my heart.

04:26.920 --> 04:35.820
And as the door of love that we just closed, tears will fall like petals when we part.

04:35.820 --> 04:41.820
I beg you to be different, but you'll always be untrue.

04:41.820 --> 04:46.820
I'm tired of forgiving, I've got nothing left to do.

04:46.820 --> 04:51.820
So I'm sending you a big bouquet of roses.

04:51.820 --> 04:57.820
One for every time you broke my heart.

04:57.820 --> 05:01.820
A big bouquet of roses.

05:01.820 --> 05:12.820
One for every time you broke my heart.

05:31.820 --> 05:58.820
So this actor...

05:58.820 --> 06:05.820
So this actor said, last week my uncle died and left me nothing.

06:05.820 --> 06:18.820
That's the end of the joke.

06:18.820 --> 06:27.820
Of course the, I mean, the joke is naturally a lot better when I can go right through it.

06:27.820 --> 06:36.820
Instead of being interrupted like, oh Bob, Bob, come here a minute, will you, just before you leave.

06:36.820 --> 06:43.820
Bob, I happened to see that show that you did with Alan Young, you know, the one you were just telling the folks about.

06:43.820 --> 06:46.820
And I thought you were wonderful. I mean, I thought it was a great show. I enjoyed it.

06:46.820 --> 06:51.820
But I mean, why would you do your encore on my first television show?

06:51.820 --> 06:56.820
I mean, why didn't you do it on his show or on your own show? I mean, Club 15.

06:56.820 --> 07:01.820
It really wasn't my idea. You see, it was my brother Everett's. He handles all of my business.

07:01.820 --> 07:04.820
Oh, you mean your brother, he's your agent.

07:04.820 --> 07:13.820
That's right. He represents me and my brother Bing and also a trained seal.

07:13.820 --> 07:14.820
You mean he handles trained seals?

07:14.820 --> 07:18.820
Well, he has one particular trained seal. This one has an act that is just wonderful.

07:18.820 --> 07:21.820
He balances a basketball on his nose. It's wonderful.

07:21.820 --> 07:22.820
Oh, really?

07:22.820 --> 07:33.820
Everett says that some jerk is going to use him on television.

07:33.820 --> 07:37.820
Well, if somebody else is going to use him, I won't want him, of course.

07:37.820 --> 07:39.820
You say your brother is an agent.

07:39.820 --> 07:41.820
That's right. He gets 10% of everything I earn.

07:41.820 --> 07:43.820
Oh, well, as an agent, he would deserve that.

07:43.820 --> 07:47.820
Well, Jack, thanks for the use of the hall. I've got to run home. We have a new baby at home.

07:47.820 --> 07:51.820
Oh, yes. For heaven's sake, I forgot all about that.

07:51.820 --> 07:53.820
Bob, congratulations.

07:53.820 --> 07:54.820
Well, thank you, Jack.

07:54.820 --> 07:56.820
That's just wonderful. How many children have you got now?

07:56.820 --> 07:57.820
Well, this makes my fifth.

07:57.820 --> 08:00.820
Five children. Wonderful.

08:00.820 --> 08:07.820
Five more, and I'm going to have to give one to Everett.

08:07.820 --> 08:10.820
Oh, that's right. He gets 10%.

08:10.820 --> 08:12.820
Oh, is that right?

08:12.820 --> 08:13.820
Would you help me?

08:13.820 --> 08:14.820
Pardon?

08:14.820 --> 08:15.820
I've got to go. Would you help me?

08:15.820 --> 08:21.820
Oh, yes.

08:21.820 --> 08:23.820
Well, it's been wonderful. Thank you, Jack.

08:23.820 --> 08:31.820
You're welcome.

08:31.820 --> 08:36.820
Big star, no tip.

08:36.820 --> 08:38.820
And now, ladies and gentlemen, I'd like to...

08:38.820 --> 08:43.820
Oh, before we go on with the show, I must tell you that tonight, or tomorrow night, rather,

08:43.820 --> 08:53.820
Mary and I are flying to New York City because the Friars Club in New York is giving me a testimonial dinner

08:53.820 --> 08:59.820
at the Waldorf Astoria Hotel to commemorate my 20th year on the radio.

08:59.820 --> 09:06.820
Just imagine, 20 years that I've been on the radio.

09:06.820 --> 09:10.820
You know, during those 20 years, I've heard so many people say...

09:10.820 --> 09:15.820
You know, at different times, I've heard people say, gee, I can't see Benny at all.

09:15.820 --> 09:21.820
Well, now they can.

09:21.820 --> 09:24.820
And now, ladies and gentlemen, I'd like to present to Jack...

09:24.820 --> 09:29.820
Oh, Don Wilson.

09:29.820 --> 09:34.820
Jack, I'm awfully sorry to be late, but my car broke down and I had to take a taxi.

09:34.820 --> 09:37.820
Well, that's all right, Don. I mean, accidents can happen, you see.

09:37.820 --> 09:40.820
Oh, but I was supposed to be here to introduce you at the very opening of the show,

09:40.820 --> 09:42.820
and I'm really awfully sorry that this happened.

09:42.820 --> 09:45.820
Don, it doesn't make any difference. The orchestra played Love and Bloom.

09:45.820 --> 09:49.820
I came on. Everybody applauded. I mean, it really doesn't matter.

09:49.820 --> 09:52.820
Oh, Jack, I had such a wonderful introduction for you.

09:52.820 --> 09:53.820
You did?

09:53.820 --> 09:54.820
Yeah.

09:54.820 --> 09:55.820
What was it?

09:55.820 --> 09:58.820
Well, this being your opening television show of the season, I thought I might say,

09:58.820 --> 10:06.820
and now, ladies and gentlemen, it is with a great deal of pride that I bring you the greatest comedian in America, Jack Benny.

10:06.820 --> 10:11.820
Oh, well, now, wait a minute, Don. Now I'm glad you were late.

10:11.820 --> 10:16.820
I mean, the greatest comedian in America. I mean, how egotistical can we get?

10:16.820 --> 10:20.820
I mean, you'd have these people hating me before the show even started.

10:20.820 --> 10:24.820
I mean, they'd tune out their television sets.

10:24.820 --> 10:26.820
The greatest comedian in America.

10:26.820 --> 10:29.820
Well, Jack, you are the greatest comedian in America, aren't you?

10:29.820 --> 10:35.820
Well, yes, but I mean, for me to say it to those people would sound awful.

10:35.820 --> 10:38.820
Well, Jack, you're not saying it. I'm saying it.

10:38.820 --> 10:40.820
Oh, oh, well, then that's different.

10:40.820 --> 10:42.820
Oh, well, then you can always introduce me like that.

10:42.820 --> 10:44.820
Oh, sure. I won't mind that.

10:44.820 --> 10:45.820
Mister.

10:45.820 --> 10:46.820
Huh?

10:46.820 --> 10:47.820
Mister.

10:47.820 --> 10:48.820
Yeah?

10:48.820 --> 10:49.820
That's you. That one.

10:49.820 --> 10:50.820
Yes?

10:50.820 --> 10:53.820
Hey, you got out of my cab in such a hurry, you forgot your briefcase.

10:53.820 --> 10:56.820
Oh, how stupid of me. Thank you. Thank you very much.

10:56.820 --> 10:59.820
Gee, I have some very, very important papers in there.

10:59.820 --> 11:03.820
Thanks a great deal, and here's a little something for your trouble.

11:03.820 --> 11:07.820
Gee, a $5 bill. Oh, thanks a lot.

11:07.820 --> 11:09.820
Well, you're certainly welcome.

11:12.820 --> 11:13.820
Don.

11:13.820 --> 11:15.820
Don, $5?

11:15.820 --> 11:20.820
Well, I mean, I can appreciate your being grateful and everything, but $5?

11:20.820 --> 11:25.820
Jack, there's nothing so strange about that. I once saw you give a man $5.

11:25.820 --> 11:30.820
Well, I know, but he was a lifeguard. He saved me from drowning.

11:30.820 --> 11:36.820
Yeah, I'll never forget how he dickered the third time that I was going down.

11:38.820 --> 11:41.820
Doc, what we carried on, really...

11:41.820 --> 11:44.820
Hey, wait a minute. Ain't you Jack Bunny?

11:44.820 --> 11:45.820
Yes.

11:45.820 --> 11:47.820
How do you like that? Don't you remember me?

11:47.820 --> 11:48.820
No.

11:48.820 --> 11:49.820
No, I don't know.

11:49.820 --> 11:53.820
I'm Harry Gilmore. You and me went to Waukegan High School together.

11:53.820 --> 11:54.820
Waukegan High School?

11:54.820 --> 11:58.820
Yeah, don't you remember? I was the one voted most likely to succeed.

11:58.820 --> 11:59.820
Oh, really?

11:59.820 --> 12:01.820
Yeah, I got my own cab.

12:03.820 --> 12:04.820
Oh, well, isn't that nice?

12:04.820 --> 12:05.820
Yeah, it certainly is.

12:05.820 --> 12:08.820
Hey, what are you doing now?

12:11.820 --> 12:14.820
I'm the voice of Bugs Bunny.

12:24.820 --> 12:26.820
I got you, but that was...

12:26.820 --> 12:29.820
So you gave up that lousy fiddle?

12:29.820 --> 12:31.820
Yes, I did.

12:33.820 --> 12:35.820
Well, goodbye, Jack. Goodbye.

12:35.820 --> 12:37.820
Goodbye, Don. So long.

12:42.820 --> 12:44.820
He's cute, isn't he?

12:44.820 --> 12:45.820
Bad for you, Donny.

12:45.820 --> 12:48.820
So this actor... Oh, I told that. I told that.

12:48.820 --> 12:52.820
And now, ladies and gentlemen, it really gives me great pleasure

12:52.820 --> 12:56.820
to present to you now the guest star of my first show,

12:56.820 --> 12:59.820
a young lady whose voice you've heard many, many times,

12:59.820 --> 13:01.820
and you've certainly heard a lot of her records,

13:01.820 --> 13:05.820
the Park Avenue hillbilly, Miss Dorothy Shea.

13:13.820 --> 13:19.820
Well, you're a hillbilly?

13:19.820 --> 13:21.820
So not funny.

13:21.820 --> 13:25.820
Well, now I know what all the feuding and fussing was about.

13:26.820 --> 13:28.820
I can't get over it. So you're a hillbilly, huh?

13:28.820 --> 13:30.820
Well, Jack, I'm not just a hillbilly.

13:30.820 --> 13:32.820
I'm the Park Avenue hillbilly.

13:32.820 --> 13:34.820
Oh, the Park Avenue hillbilly, huh?

13:34.820 --> 13:38.820
Well, you can park on my avenue anytime.

13:41.820 --> 13:44.820
I thought that was a pretty good ad lib, didn't you?

13:44.820 --> 13:45.820
Ad lib? What's that?

13:45.820 --> 13:48.820
Well, an ad lib is when you say something impromptu,

13:48.820 --> 13:51.820
you know, without thinking, just something in the spur of the moment.

13:51.820 --> 13:52.820
See what I mean?

13:52.820 --> 13:56.820
No, but I accept your apology.

13:56.820 --> 13:59.820
Well, now, look, Dorothy, what do you want to sing for your first number?

13:59.820 --> 14:01.820
What's the name of the song you're going to sing?

14:01.820 --> 14:04.820
Well, don't you think we should discuss salary?

14:04.820 --> 14:07.820
That's a wonderful title. Thank you very, very much.

14:07.820 --> 14:11.820
Jack, Jack.

14:11.820 --> 14:13.820
Oh, well.

14:13.820 --> 14:19.820
You can have your New York City with its swank Park Avenue.

14:19.820 --> 14:24.820
You can have your gilded mansions in Westchester.

14:24.820 --> 14:29.820
You can have your own cabana in Miami or Havana,

14:29.820 --> 14:34.820
where the bluebloods congregate for their siesta.

14:34.820 --> 14:40.820
You can have your fancy penthouse on some 42nd floor,

14:40.820 --> 14:46.820
but I'll guarantee wherever you may roam,

14:46.820 --> 14:49.820
with the tinsel and the glare,

14:49.820 --> 14:52.820
why, those places can't compare

14:52.820 --> 15:00.820
with the humble little spot I call my home.

15:00.820 --> 15:07.820
It's a little western town called Beverly Hills,

15:07.820 --> 15:11.820
where people live a life that's free from cares.

15:11.820 --> 15:16.820
No one struggles to be great. All they ask is an even break.

15:16.820 --> 15:20.820
They're satisfied to just be millionaires.

15:20.820 --> 15:25.820
Every housewife is contented with her lot,

15:25.820 --> 15:29.820
and some of them don't even own a yacht.

15:29.820 --> 15:34.820
She may have just one swimming pool, one husband to caress her.

15:34.820 --> 15:40.820
She's bored to death with Cadillacs and diamonds just to caress her.

15:40.820 --> 15:47.820
She'd rather have some trinkets, like that Oscar on her dresser.

15:47.820 --> 15:51.820
In that little western town called Beverly Hills.

15:51.820 --> 15:56.820
In that little western town called Beverly Hills.

15:56.820 --> 16:00.820
You're welcome as the flowers are blooming May.

16:00.820 --> 16:05.820
No one cares about your pants or inquires about your pants.

16:05.820 --> 16:09.820
If you only have one bumper, that's okay.

16:09.820 --> 16:14.820
The children ring your bell on Halloween,

16:14.820 --> 16:18.820
then beat it in a chauffeured limousine.

16:18.820 --> 16:23.820
Each mother with a little son entrusted to her care

16:23.820 --> 16:29.820
will gently rock his cradle as she offers up a prayer.

16:29.820 --> 16:37.820
That someday he'll be president of Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer.

16:37.820 --> 16:41.820
In that little western town called Beverly Hills.

16:41.820 --> 17:07.820
There's a little racing in that little western town called Beverly Hills.

17:07.820 --> 17:09.820
Thank you ever so much.

17:09.820 --> 17:13.820
I thought you folks might be interested in meeting some of my kinfolk.

17:13.820 --> 17:16.820
So with your permission we'd like to take you down to the Ozarks

17:16.820 --> 17:23.820
and meet my favorite cousin, Zeke Benny and his mad mountain boys.

17:23.820 --> 17:40.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

17:40.820 --> 17:53.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

17:53.820 --> 18:18.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

18:18.820 --> 18:36.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

18:36.820 --> 18:40.820
You are my sunshine, my only sunshine.

18:40.820 --> 18:45.820
You make me happy when skies are gray.

18:45.820 --> 18:49.820
You'll never know dear how much I love you.

18:49.820 --> 18:53.820
So please don't take my sunshine away.

18:53.820 --> 19:15.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

19:15.820 --> 19:24.820
ZEKE BENNY AND HIS MOUNTAIN BOYS

19:24.820 --> 19:27.820
I want to thank you folks.

19:27.820 --> 19:29.820
I'd like to have you meet some of the boys.

19:29.820 --> 19:32.820
You'll meet a fellow over here with a concertina.

19:32.820 --> 19:36.820
That's Charlie Bagby. He's the smart one of the troupe here.

19:36.820 --> 19:39.820
Hey Charlie, how much is two and two?

19:39.820 --> 19:46.820
CHARLIE BAGBY

19:46.820 --> 19:49.820
He's our business manager.

19:49.820 --> 19:52.820
The fellow here with the clarinet is Wayne Songer.

19:52.820 --> 19:54.820
We're mighty proud of Wayne.

19:54.820 --> 20:00.820
Last week at the county fair he won first prize at the fly attraction contest.

20:00.820 --> 20:07.820
Wasn't even entered.

20:07.820 --> 20:24.820
This is my wife.

20:24.820 --> 20:34.820
This is our boy, Sammy.

20:34.820 --> 20:36.820
Get up there, Frank.

20:36.820 --> 20:43.820
This is Frankie Remley.

20:43.820 --> 20:46.820
Remley's been associated with Phil Harris for about 20 years.

20:46.820 --> 20:50.820
Glad to see you standing up, Frank.

20:50.820 --> 20:52.820
Never took a guitar lesson in his life.

20:52.820 --> 20:55.820
Just took it up by accident.

20:55.820 --> 20:57.820
He figured as long as his hand was shaking anyway,

20:57.820 --> 21:01.820
he might as well put a guitar on.

21:01.820 --> 21:03.820
He's the fellow that makes us laugh all the time.

21:03.820 --> 21:04.820
He's a car.

21:04.820 --> 21:06.820
Say something funny, Frankie.

21:06.820 --> 21:20.820
Rage of a Tad!

21:20.820 --> 21:21.820
And now folks...

21:21.820 --> 21:22.820
Hey, Lem!

21:22.820 --> 21:24.820
Now listen, Lem.

21:24.820 --> 21:26.820
I got a bone to pick with you.

21:26.820 --> 21:29.820
I don't like the way you're treating my daughter.

21:29.820 --> 21:30.820
I thought...

21:30.820 --> 21:32.820
You took her out last night.

21:32.820 --> 21:34.820
Well, I brought her by.

21:34.820 --> 21:47.820
That's what I mean!

21:47.820 --> 21:50.820
For our next number, we're going to play a real hot tune

21:50.820 --> 21:53.820
called Fascinating Rhythm.

21:53.820 --> 22:22.820
Take it, boys.

22:22.820 --> 22:51.820
Hit it, boys!

22:51.820 --> 23:01.820
Turn!

23:21.820 --> 23:31.820
Turn!

